  "query_preview": "..\nThrough the Weekend...\n\nA much less active weather pattern is in store for the better part\nof the week. As a weak low continues to shift off into the\nNorthern Plains through the day today, the ridge over the\nIntermountain West will build back into our region. This will\nbeing back weak winds, warm temperatures, and the potential for\nmonsoonal moisture. The temperatures through the first half of the\nweek will remain seasonal with a slow steady ramp up towards\nThursday and Friday where a lot of the CWA will see highs in the\nlow 90s again. Low level winds will remain out of the west /\ndownslope through the week keeping RH values low, with the lowest\nvalues coming late week with the higher temps. \n\nAlthough the ridge will dominate the weather across the region, we\nwill be close enough to the apex of the feature that we will be\nimpacted by weak vort maxes that push over the ridge. It looks\nlike each day we will see one of these small features push up out\nof the Snake River Plain into SW Montana. This will give our\nMontana mountains a decent chance for afternoon showers and storms\neach day this week. Tuesday looks like there will be a slightly \nstronger low which will provide better chances for precipitation \nas well as seeing some showers move off into the immediate \nfoothills for places like Livingston and Red Lodge. \n\nThursday will see a different setup as the ridge begins to break \ndown just slightly. An upper level low over BC will eject a lobe \nof energy through Montana which will give all of the CWA a chance \nfor precipitation Thursday afternoon and evening. Surface winds \nadvecting in moisture out of the plains combined with the low of Pacific\norigins will provide enough moisture for there to be locally \nheavy rainfall. \n\nLooking ahead to the weekend, that low over off the Pacific coast\nwill be pushed inland and through our area. This will bring \ncooler temperatures and a chance for widespread precipitation. \nThis setup looks like it may also bring some winds due to the \nprogressin and orientation of the low, however the modeled low \nlevel surface winds are not too strong as of now. After this \nweekend's low, the long range models keep the pattern fairly \nactive and progressive through the first week of September. WMR",
